<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>(a) Vision-based Offloading Strategy in Different Degree of Noise.
(b) Kinematic Offloading Strategy Performance.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>2 </span><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correlations between Vision-Based Confidence and Kinematics</span></h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome the problem of environment-oriented strategies, we shift our focus from visual inputs to proprioceptive states.
We tested various kinematic indicators, among which the instantaneous joint acceleration and Joint Torque were the least susceptible to external environmental noise interference.
The instantaneous joint acceleration <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time step <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be derived via finite difference from the joint velocities <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{q}</annotation></semantics></math>,where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math> is the control interval:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{q}_{t}=\frac{\dot{q}_{t}-\dot{q}_{t-1}}{\Delta t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{q}_{t}=\frac{\dot{q}_{t}-\dot{q}_{t-1}}{\Delta t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The corresponding joint torque <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is inherently governed by the rigid-body dynamics of the manipulator,where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is the mass/inertia matrix, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(q,\dot{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(q,\dot{q})</annotation></semantics></math> accounts for Coriolis and centrifugal forces, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(q)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes gravity, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ext}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ext}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the external interaction torques:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=M(q)\ddot{q}+C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q}+G(q)+\tau_{ext}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=M(q)\ddot{q}+C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q}+G(q)+\tau_{ext}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in the top panel of Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-A1 Compatibility Limitations of Environment-Oriented strategies Across Multi-Environments ‣ III-A Compatibility Analysis of Partitioning Schemes ‣ III Observation and Motivation ‣ RAPID: Redundancy-Aware and Compatibility-Optimal Edge-Cloud Partitioned Inference for Diverse VLA models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, vision-based entropy is highly susceptible to environmental disturbances.
Under conditions of visual noise or external distractions, the entropy frequently breaches the offloading threshold during routine movements (e.g., the Approach Phase).
This instability leads to severe action interruptions and inefficient cloud offloads, wasting computational resources.
Meanwhile, due to the high threshold setting, the entropy values in standard(noise-free) scenarios never exceed the threshold. Consequently, all computations are executed on the edge, resulting in severe resource waste.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike visual observations, proprioceptive data directly reflects the physical state of the embodied agent and remains immune to external visual noise.
As demonstrated in the bottom panel of Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ RAPID: Redundancy-Aware and Compatibility-Optimal Edge-Cloud Partitioned Inference for Diverse VLA models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, kinematic features such as instantaneous acceleration and joint torque maintain near-zero variance during the approach phase, basically unaffected by visual noise. Crucially, these metrics exhibit distinct, sharp peaks exclusively during the Critical Interaction phase, reliably crossing the kinematic threshold to trigger efficient offloads.
Therefore, we propose utilizing these inherent kinematic features.
Specifically, instantaneous acceleration can rapidly and accurately identify critical interactions during the high-speed stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Insight ①: Focusing on the robot itself and using kinematics to partition computation between edge and cloud offers superior compatibility compared to vision-based approaches.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07949v1/3.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/334;" width="476" height="334" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Correlation Analysis between Joint Torque and Step-Wise Redundancy</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Redundancy Analysis During Multi-Step Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Motivation ②:
Existing computing partitioning strategies often ignore the unique step redundancy inherent in VLA models.
This oversight leads to redundant computations and suboptimal edge-cloud collaboration performance, prompting us to leverage this redundancy for system optimization.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step-Wise Redundancy Identification in VLA Inference</span></h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantitative analysis of attention weights across various embodied tasks reveals significant disparities in attention distribution, demonstrating that the importance of actions varies during task execution.
As shown in Tab. <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-B1 Step-Wise Redundancy Identification in VLA Inference ‣ III-B Redundancy Analysis During Multi-Step Generation ‣ III Observation and Motivation ‣ RAPID: Redundancy-Aware and Compatibility-Optimal Edge-Cloud Partitioned Inference for Diverse VLA models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, substantial actions demonstrate step-wise redundancy with remarkably low mean attention weights (ranging from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.005</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.005</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.008" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.008</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.008</annotation></semantics></math>).
The redundant actions account for more than 80%. making them highly suitable for real-time inference and control at the edge.
Conversely, critical actions with significantly elevated weights should be offloaded to the cloud.
So, Step-wise redundancy identifies action importance, making it an ideal strategy for edge-cloud partitioning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Quantitative Analysis of Attention Distribution and Action Redundancy</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:62.9pt;vertical-align:-28.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(3.7pt,-0.7pt) scale(1.02220456804538,1.02220456804538) ;">
<table id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_ttt" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Domain</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_ttt" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequence Info</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_ttt" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action Ratio</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_ttt" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean Attn Weight</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/L</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{red}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{crit}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{crit}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{red}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{crit}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{crit}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">Pick &amp; Place</th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">50</th>
