</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The advantages of RAPID are particularly evident during real-world deployment on physical manipulators, where execution fluency is paramount. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ VI-B2 Results on Real-World Environments ‣ VI-B Main Results ‣ VI Experiments ‣ RAPID: Redundancy-Aware and Compatibility-Optimal Edge-Cloud Partitioned Inference for Diverse VLA models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, conventional methods exhibit sub-optimal latency trade-offs: the Edge-Only VLA baseline incurs a prohibitive total latency of 812.6 ms, while the vision-based dynamic routing (ISAR) yields 414.1 ms. By optimally partitioning the computational workload, RAPID maintains a minimal edge footprint of only 2.4 GB while offloading 12.1 GB to the cloud. This efficient edge-cloud collaborative paradigm reduces the total latency to 239.7 ms, achieving an end-to-end system speedup of approximately <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.73\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.73</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.73\times</annotation></semantics></math> over the vision-based baseline, thereby demonstrating its efficacy for latency-critical robotic control.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Studies</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the contribution of our dual-threshold mechanism, we conduct ablation experiments on the LIBERO suite. We compare the full <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RAPID</span> framework against two variants: (1) <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{comp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{comp}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which removes the compatibility-optimal (acceleration-based) trigger, and (2) <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{red}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which removes the redundancy-aware (torque-based) trigger.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Tab. <a href="#S6.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ VI-C Ablation Studies ‣ VI Experiments ‣ RAPID: Redundancy-Aware and Compatibility-Optimal Edge-Cloud Partitioned Inference for Diverse VLA models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>,removing <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{comp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{comp}</annotation></semantics></math> (Acc.) results in an increased total latency of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="280.9\pm 22.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>280.9</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>22.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">280.9\pm 22.7</annotation></semantics></math>ms, as the edge device is burdened with <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="228.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>228.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">228.5</annotation></semantics></math>ms of latency and a <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.0</annotation></semantics></math>GB load. Conversely, removing <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{red}</annotation></semantics></math> (Torque) increases the total latency even further to <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="315.6\pm 32.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>315.6</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>32.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">315.6\pm 32.4</annotation></semantics></math>ms, heavily overwhelming the edge device with <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="270.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>270.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">270.4</annotation></semantics></math>ms of latency and a <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.7</annotation></semantics></math>GB load. Our dual-threshold partitioning method, <span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RAPID (Ours)</span>, effectively optimizes this balance. By successfully offloading more processing to the cloud-side (handling an <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.8</annotation></semantics></math>GB load with <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>83.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.5</annotation></semantics></math>ms latency), it significantly reduces the edge-side burden to just <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="139.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>139.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">139.4</annotation></semantics></math>ms and <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4</annotation></semantics></math>GB, ultimately achieving the lowest total latency of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="222.9\pm 11.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>222.9</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>11.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">222.9\pm 11.4</annotation></semantics></math>ms while maintaining a constant total load of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.2</annotation></semantics></math>GB.</p>
</div>
